problem_statement:
Missing parentheses on reference applied to a cast 
```c
int main(void) {
    int * x = &(int){0};
}
```

generates

```rust
#![allow(dead_code, mutable_transmutes, non_camel_case_types, non_snake_case, non_upper_case_globals, unused_assignments, unused_mut)]
unsafe fn main_0() -> libc::c_int {
    let mut x: *mut libc::c_int = &mut 0 as libc::c_int as *mut libc::c_int;
    return 0;
}
pub fn main() {
    unsafe { ::std::process::exit(main_0() as i32) }
}
```

but extra parentheses are needed

```rust
    let mut x: *mut libc::c_int = &mut (0 as libc::c_int) as *mut libc::c_int;
```

This can happen in more realistic code when an output function parameter is being ignored `f(&(int){0});`

If someone reads this issue and wonders if this is even legal C, check out [compound literal](https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/language/compound_literal)

> The value category of a compound literal is [lvalue](https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/language/value_category) (its address can be taken). ...

