problem_statement:
rs-port: clarify how to capture variables from the surrounding env in `MetacallFutureHandler`
## Description
According to using `fn(...)` instead of `impl Fn(...) -> ()` due to lifetime issues, We cannot use closures to capture the variables from the surrounding env because that's impossible in function points. 
https://github.com/metacall/core/blob/9632ba27cd1819f2e100c84c531485dfc7c95a7e/source/ports/rs_port/src/types/metacall_future.rs#L16

The documentation doesn't offer details about passing data to the future and using it in the handlers.


**Example for trying to capture a variable from the surrounding env:**
```rust
use metacall::{MetacallValue, MetacallFuture, metacall};
 
fn run() {
  let x = 10;
  fn resolve(result: impl MetacallValue, data: impl MetacallValue) {
      println!("X = {x}");
  }
   
  fn reject(result: impl MetacallValue, data: impl MetacallValue) {
      println!("X = {x}");
  }
    
  let future = metacall::<MetacallFuture>("async_function", [1]).unwrap();
  future.then(resolve).catch(reject).await_fut();
}
```

**Error:**
```
error[E0434]: can't capture dynamic environment in a fn item
 --> src/run.rs:7:23
  |
7 |       println!("X = {x}");
  |                         ^ cannot capture dynamic environment in a fn item
  |
  = help: use the `|| { ... }` closure form instead
```

## Solution
There's a method used to solve this problem, [`MetacallFuture.data(data: impl MetacallValue)`](https://github.com/metacall/core/blob/9632ba27cd1819f2e100c84c531485dfc7c95a7e/source/ports/rs_port/src/types/metacall_future.rs#L158C27-L158C52), but the documentation doesn't clarify what its benefit is and when we should use it. Users may be confused about what this method is, and how they can pass data from the surrounding environment to use it instead of using closures. So, I suggest improving this method's documentation with some examples.

