problem_statement:
Slow scrolling on flickable is hard (when there is no toucharea inside) 
### Bug Description

I just noticed on out target devices with resistive and capacitive touch, that slow scrolling fails most of the time. Slow scrolling will likely happen because we have a small screen. I think the cursor speed is still reasonable.

The behaviour can be improved when there is a `TouchArea` inside. Check out the code below and the attached video. The column on the right has `TouchArea`s.


[Scrolling.webm](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/dc09422f-237e-4835-91f1-1e32565b602a)

I also took a short look how firefox and chrome handle scrolling. They both have not problem with slow scrolling. Imo chrome handles touch scroll in the best way (I think safari is similiar). What I like about it is, that chrome directly sends the pressed event to an underlying button and if you move the cursor over some threshold, then it starts scrolling. It seems there is also no timer involved, as you can wait a few seconds and then start scrolling.



### Reproducible Code (if applicable)

```slint
export component Example inherits Window {
    width: 300px;
    height: 500px;

    HorizontalLayout {
    spacing: 20px;
        Flickable {
            VerticalLayout {
                for i in 20: Rectangle {
                    background: @linear-gradient(0deg, red, green);
                    height: 52px;
                }
            }
        }
        Flickable {
            VerticalLayout {
                for i in 20: Rectangle {
                    TouchArea {}
                    background: @linear-gradient(0deg, red, green);
                    height: 52px;
                }
                
            }
        }
    }
}
```


### Environment Details

- Slint Version: 1.8.0



### Product Impact

Critical, as scrolling fails most of the time.

